  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "In this question we will prove the surprising fact that $(0,1)$ and $(0,\\infty)$ have the same cardinality. Let $f:(0,1)\\to(0,\\infty)$ be defined by\n$$f(x)=\\frac{x}{1-x}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p38-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of $f$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "An increasing curve on $0<x<1$, with an open endpoint at $(0,0)$ and vertical asymptote $x=1$.",
          "worked": "For $0<x<1$, $f(x)>0$. The curve approaches $(0,0)$ as $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, but excludes this point. As $x$ approaches $1$ from the left, $f(x)$ increases without bound. It passes through $\\left(\\frac12,1\\right)$ and has vertical asymptote $x=1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p38-q6-a-solution-graph",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"teacher PDF page 43, Section I question 6(a)\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=3cm,y=0.75cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-0.35,-0.65) rectangle (1.65,5.8);\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (-0.3,0)--(1.5,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,-0.6)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};\n\\draw[dashed,line width=0.4pt] (1,-0.55)--(1,5.4);\n\\draw[line width=0.8pt,domain=0:0.84,samples=100] plot (\\x,{\\x/(1-\\x)});\n\\draw[fill=white,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) circle[radius=1.1mm];\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 55,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
              "spec": {
                "sourcePage": 38,
                "sourceRegion": "Question 6(a); teacher PDF page 43, lower-right graph",
                "description": "Restricted rational-function graph with an open origin, increasing positive branch and dashed vertical asymptote. The source has no numeric ticks or grid.",
                "mathematics": "y=x/(1-x), 0<x<1; vertical asymptote x=1; excluded endpoint (0,0).",
                "answerVisibility": "Answer editions only."
              },
              "derived": false,
              "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
            }
          ]
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that $f$ is injective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is injective.\n\nEquating two outputs and cross-multiplying gives equal inputs.",
          "worked": "Let $u,v\\in(0,1)$ and suppose $f(u)=f(v)$. The denominators are non-zero, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{u}{1-u}&=\\frac{v}{1-v}\\\\\nu(1-v)&=v(1-u)\\\\\nu-uv&=v-uv\\\\\nu&=v.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $f$ is surjective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f$ is surjective: every $y>0$ has the preimage $x=\\frac{y}{1+y}\\in(0,1)$.",
          "worked": "Let $y\\in(0,\\infty)$. Solve $f(x)=y$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nf(x)&=y\\\\\n\\frac{x}{1-x}&=y\\\\\nx&=y(1-x)\\\\\nx&=y-xy\\\\\nx+xy&=y\\\\\nx(1+y)&=y\\\\\nx&=\\frac{y}{1+y}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nSince $y>0$, we have $0<\\frac{y}{1+y}<1$. For this input,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(\\frac{y}{1+y}\\right)&=\\frac{\\frac{y}{1+y}}{1-\\frac{y}{1+y}}\\\\\nf\\left(\\frac{y}{1+y}\\right)&=y.\n\\end{align*}$$\nEvery element of the codomain therefore has a preimage in the domain, so $f$ is surjective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 75
      }
    ]
  },
